# S02 · Who Chooses the Last Citizens’ Assembly?

[Seven essays](../README.md) · [Current selection](../../../docs/selection.md) · [After the midterm](../../../docs/post-midterm.md)

A mayor keeps commissioning citizens' assemblies until one approves his policy, then presents that panel's report as the consultation's answer. Every panel may have been fairly sampled and free to deliberate. The essay asks what justifies giving the conveniently favorable report that special status.

[Read R1](R1.md) · [Original draft](draft.md) · [Argument critique](critique-A.md) · [Literature critique](critique-B.md) · [Author response](author-response.md)

## The resulting argument

R1 separates an authentic participant judgment, evidence about wider judgments, and a report's institutional role. Fair sampling alone does not justify transferring that role to the selected final report: the rules for continuing, stopping, and choosing reports also need an account. Repeated consultation can be justified through a separate mandate or argument; the essay identifies what the appeal to fair sampling leaves unanswered.

## Production record

S02 grew from I012, the sole tentative seed admitted from the 300-attempt search. A fresh author received that seed and neutral rules. It then completed the [shared author–two-critics–revision cycle](../README.md#the-shared-writing-cycle): both critics read the same full draft, and a fresh editor wrote the response and R1 after their reports were archived.

**Recorded settings:** GPT-6.1 Sol/max for author, both critics, and editor; Standard, under the separately recorded October 15 authorization.

**Accepted release:** October 15, 2026 · 14:50 PDT (`2026-10-15T21:50:36.486206+00:00`). [Source fingerprints and release metadata](metadata.json) · [Shared provenance and review scope](../README.md#the-shared-writing-cycle).
